  "narrative": "February 2026 produced 32 food-safety hazard recall incidents across the AFTS monitoring network, a -11% move below the prior month — the rolling baseline is too narrow for a Z estimate this early in the series. Salmonella spp. dominated with 17 of 32 incidents (53%). The composite severity index closed at 60.0/100 (elevated), with 30 Tier-1 critical events and 3 outbreak-associated events (no temporal clustering) on record.\n\nStructurally, the month reads as geographically concentrated with mixed signal concentration (Source HHI 1738.0, Geographic Gini 0.414). For a Salmonella spp.-dominated month, the relevant failure modes are Listeria persistence on food-contact surfaces and in associated equipment niches, followed by post-process contamination of RTE foods, together with sanitation SOP drift and cold-chain lapses. These are the leading hypotheses for a Salmonella spp.-dominated month; inadequate process delivery or other control failures cannot be excluded without event-specific evidence. The Tier-1 intensity ratio of 0.96× vs the rolling baseline indicates severity is in line.\n\nLooking forward, operators in Salmonella spp.-relevant commodity categories should re-verify the single highest-leverage control this month: environmental monitoring swab frequency on RTE deli and dairy lines, or product-specific thermal lethality validation for low-moisture commodities — measured under the actual water activity, formulation and process conditions rather than a generic D-value — whichever matches their product mix. Documentation packages should be ready for rapid regulatory response given continued inspection intensity.\n\nProcess Authority Note: This window includes Clostridium botulinum or botulism-relevant recalls. The control review for such events is product-specific. For shelf-stable low-acid, acidified, aseptic, or commercially sterile products, scheduled-process adequacy under a qualified process authority is the central control. For refrigerated anaerobic products — peeled garlic, sous-vide, vacuum or modified-atmosphere packs, smoked or fermented fish — the operative controls are validated refrigeration discipline, water-activity, pH, salt content, oxygen barrier integrity, and shelf-life qualification, with or without a thermal step. Across global jurisdictions the regulatory architecture differs but the engineering principle converges. In the United States, FDA 21 CFR 113 (thermally processed low-acid canned foods) and 21 CFR 114 (acidified foods) require a scheduled process established by a qualified process authority. In the European Union, Regulation 852/2004 imposes general hygiene and HACCP obligations on food-business operators, and Regulation 2073/2005 fixes microbiological criteria — neither is a direct equivalent of US LACF/acidified-food filing, but both presuppose process control validated against a documented hazard analysis, with national LACF/acidified-food rules layered on top in some Member States. Canada's Safe Food for Canadians Regulations, FSA / FSS in the UK, FSANZ in Australia and New Zealand, and Japan's MHLW each impose comparable preventive-control obligations. The common engineering principle: low-acid, aseptic, and commercially sterile products require a scheduled process established by a qualified process authority and executed under documented GMP oversight. Recall events in this category most commonly trace to one of: an unfiled or outdated scheduled process; a deviation resolved without qualified review; a formulation or packaging change introduced without process re-evaluation; a temperature-abuse event in the cold chain; or seal-integrity loss in anaerobic packaging. Manufacturers producing low-acid canned, acidified, fermented, or anaerobically packaged products — including refrigerated anaerobic products — should confirm with a qualified process authority that filings, multi-hurdle controls, GMP programmes, and deviation handling remain current before any production change.",
